.03216552734375, -0.01560211181640625, 0.0299072265625, -0.00853729248046875, -0.005687713623046875, -0.0031185150146484375, 0.01097869873046875, 0.0201568603515625, 0.01129150390625, -0.055206298828125, -0.01314544677734375, -0.01390838623046875, -0.006450653076171875, 0.007144927978515625, 0.002986907958984375, 0.01092529296875, -0.0306243896484375, 0.00708770751953125, -0.004428863525390625, -0.01373291015625, -0.0023059844970703125, -0.0256500244140625, -0.038116455078125, 0.0059967041015625, 0.0183868408203125, -0.005977630615234375, 0.01690673828125, 0.016387939453125, -0.010101318359375, 0.00981903076171875, -0.01519775390625, -0.0243377685546875, -0.021697998046875, 0.016021728515625, 0.00786590576171875, -0.0230712890625, 0.0084228515625, 0.01509857177734375, -0.014312744140625, 0.0240020751953125, 0.0077362060546875, -0.0112152099609375, 0.01398468017578125, -0.00592803955078125, 0.02325439453125, -0.0030536651611328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, existing methods\u2014such as global order parameters or traditional phase coherence measures\u2014often struggle to capture transient dynamics or to distinguish between different spatial organizations when global synchronization levels are similar. For example, global synchrony and traveling waves can yield comparable synchronization indices, and short-lived or spatially localized patterns may be missed by time-averaged metrics. Identifying and tracking these patterns as they evolve remains a significant challenge, particularly in the presence of complex or short-lived dynamics.\n\nHere, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. In this approach, we focus on the phase relationships between neighboring oscillators, rather than their amplitudes. Specifically, for each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors and encode the local ordering as a symbolic pattern. To handle cases where phases are nearly equal\u2014situations that standard ordinal patterns may not distinguish\u2014we introduce special symbols to represent ties. A threshold for \"nearly equal\" phases is set based on a small fixed value (e.g., a fraction of \u03c0), chosen to be robust to noise yet sensitive to meaningful differences; this threshold can be tuned depending on the system and noise level. This ensures that both phase gradients and synchronized clusters are captured within a single framework.\n\nFrom this symbolic representation, we define a spatial permutation entropy, which quantifies the diversity of spatial ordinal patterns present at each time point. This entropy is computed by evaluating the probability distribution of the observed patterns across the network at each time step, typically normalized by the maximum possible entropy for the given pattern size. The probability distribution is estimated over the spatial ensemble of oscillators at each time point, rather than over a temporal window, allowing for the detection of rapid changes. High entropy indicates a wide variety of local phase configurations, while low entropy reflects more uniform or ordered states. This enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach can distinguish between phase-locked states that have identical levels of global synchronization but differ in their spatial organization, such as uniform synchrony versus traveling waves. In practice, uniform synchrony yields a dominance of a single ordinal pattern (all phases equal or nearly equal), resulting in low entropy, while traveling waves produce a consistent gradient in phase across space, leading to a different dominant pattern or a broader distribution of patterns. Additionally, the method effectively characterizes partially synchronized states, where clusters of oscillators are synchronized locally but not globally.\n\nWe validate the method on synthetic oscillator networks, including both regular lattice and random network topologies, across multiple spatiotemporal regimes. Furthermore, we apply the framework to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers. EEG data were preprocessed using standard artifact rejection and bandpass filtering, and cognitive or behavioral conditions were distinguished based on experimental task labels and changes in the spatial permutation entropy profiles within individual subjects. These results highlight the potential of our approach for revealing subtle and transient spatiotemporal patterns in both model and real-world oscillatory systems.\n\nWhile our method is robust to moderate noise and applicable across different network topologies, it assumes reliable phase estimation and may be sensitive to high noise levels or very sparse connectivity. Future work could explore adaptive thresholding for tie detection, extensions to weighted or directed networks, and applications to other types of spatiotemporal data, broadening the utility of spatial ordinal pattern analysis in complex systems."}}